   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to increasing the membership of the marine and coastal district of New York conservation, education, and research board and to amend part LL of chapter 59 of the laws of 2009 amending the environmental conservation law relating to establishing a recreational marine fishing license and setting the fees for such license, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S6110",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6110-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0503, En Con L; amd Part LL §8, Chap 59 of 2009",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6110\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\nincreasing the membership of the marine and coastal district of New\nYork conservation, education, and research board and to amend part LL\nof chapter 59 of the laws of 2009 amending the environmental\nconservation law relating to establishing a recreational marine\nfishing license and setting the fees for such license, in relation to\nthe effectiveness thereof\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nThe purpose of this legislation is to expand the Marine and Coastal\nDistrict of New York Conservation, Education and Research Board to six\nmembers, and to clarify that the first license year of the\nrecreational marine fishing license begins on January 1, 2010.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection one amends section 13-0503 of the Environmental Conservation\nLaw by expanding the Marine and Coastal District of New York\nConservation, Education and Research Board to six members.\n\nSection two amends Section 8 of part LL of Chapter 59 of the laws of\n2009 amending the Environmental Conservation Law to clarify that the\neffective date of the marine fishing license will be January 1, 2010.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe Marine and Coastal District of New York Conservation, Education\nand Research Board currently has only three members. With the recent\nenactment of a marine fishing license, the board will have\nsignificantly more responsibilities. This legislation expands the\nboard from three to six members, thereby giving them the resources\nneeded to fulfill their duties.  This legislation also clarifies that\nthe license year for the marine fishing license begins on January 1,\n2010.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
